.\" Man page for the Improv CLI.
.TH IMPROV 1 "2026-10-20" "improv 0.10.0" "Improv Manual"
.SH NAME
improv \- headless multidimensional spreadsheet model CLI
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B improv
.I command
.RI [ args ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B improv
is the command-line front-end to the Improv multidimensional spreadsheet.
It operates on a model store backed by an embedded (SQLite) Mentat datom
database, or, when
.I <db>
is a
.BI postgres:// ...?model= name
(or
.BR postgresql:// )
URL, on that model in a Postgres database's
.B improv
catalog. Each invocation opens the store, mutates the loaded model, and saves
it back.
.PP
A model is built from
.B categories
(dimensions such as Time or Product),
.B items
(members of a category, such as 2025 or WidgetA),
.B measures
(named variables indexed by a subset of the categories), and
.B input cells
(raw values at a coordinate). A coordinate maps category names to item names.
.PP
The
.B \-\-at
flag on
.B set
and
.B scenario
names a coordinate as
.IR Category = Item
pairs, resolved against the loaded model. Pairs may be comma-joined
.RB ( "\-\-at Time=Q1,Product=A" ),
space-separated after one flag
.RB ( "\-\-at Time=Q1 Product=A" ),
or spread over repeated flags
.RB ( "\-\-at Time=Q1 \-\-at Product=A" );
all are merged. Naming a category twice is an error, as is any argument that
is not a pair after
.BR \-\-at .
Numeric ids are given directly on the command line.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init \fI<db>\fR
Create or open the store at
.IR <db> ,
and install the schema. An existing model is left untouched. Use an empty
string for an in-memory database.
.TP
.B add-category \fI<db> <id> <name>\fR
Add a category (dimension).
.I <id>
is a number.
.TP
.B add-item \fI<db> <id> <category-id> <name>\fR
Add an item (a member of a category).
.I <id>
and
.I <category-id>
are numbers. The category must already exist.
.TP
.B add-measure \fI<db> <id> <name>\fR number|boolean|text\fR input\fR [\fICategory ...\fR]
Add an input measure.
.I <id>
is a number. The type keyword
.RB ( number ,
.BR boolean ,
or
.BR text )
declares how
.B set
parses values for this measure. The trailing
.B input
keyword is required. Any further arguments are category
.I names
declaring the dimensions this measure ranges over, e.g.
.BR "input Time Product" .
.TP
.B add-derived \fI<db> <id> <name> <formula>\fR
Add a derived (formula) measure. The
.I <formula>
is Improv formula text, e.g.
.BR \(dqPrice * Quantity\(dq
or
.BR \(dqSUM(Revenue OVER Time)\(dq ,
with measure and category names resolved against the model. The measure's
categories are inferred from the measures it references.
.TP
.B define \fI<db> <id> \(dq<Target = ...>\(dq\fR
Create a measure from a definition string whose left side is the target name.
Three forms: an ordinary formula (
.BR \(dqRevenue = Price * Quantity\(dq ),
an external call (
.BR \(dqH = CALL(hypot, Price, Quantity)\(dq ),
which registers an external-function measure populated by
.BR refresh-ext ,
or a named LAMBDA (
.BR \(dqMargin = LAMBDA(p, c, p - c)\(dq ),
which formulas then call as
.BR Margin(Price, Cost) ;
.I id
names the lambda, and redefining a name keeps its first id.
A
.B CALL(...)
measure also takes
.B \-\-refresh
.RI manual | on-load | interval: SECS
(when
.B serve-refresh
re-runs it).
.TP
.B register-ext \fI<db> <name> <arity> \(dq<python body>\(dq\fR
Register a pure Python external function (Number arguments and result). The
body binds a list
.I args
and sets
.IR result .
.TP
.B refresh-ext \fI<db> <measure-id>\fR
Re-populate a
.BR CALL(...)
measure by running its function over its argument measures, host-side
(external calls stay off the differential-dataflow evaluation path).
.TP
.B set \fI<db> <measure-id> <value>\fR [\fB\-\-at\fR \fICat=Item,Cat=Item ...\fR]
Set an input cell. The
.I <value>
is parsed by the measure's declared type (a float for
.BR number ,
.B true
or
.B false
for
.BR boolean ,
verbatim text for
.BR text ).
.B \-\-at
maps category names to item names; omit it for a scalar cell.
.TP
.B list \fI<db>\fR
Print all categories, items, measures, saved views, and input cells.
.TP
.B save\-view \fI<db> <view-id> <name> <measure>\fR [\fB\-\-rows\fR \fICat,Cat\fR] [\fB\-\-cols\fR \fICat,...\fR]
Save a named view of
.I <measure>
(an id or a name), the same shape the GUI's Save View writes.
.B \-\-rows
and
.B \-\-cols
stack category names outer-to-inner; each defaults to the next unused
category of the measure. The remaining categories become pages, pinned to
their first item. A view with the same id is replaced.
.TP
.B show \fI<db> <measure-id>\fR
Print one measure and its input cells.
.TP
.B eval \fI<db> <measure-id>\fR [\fB\-\-scenario\fR \fIname\fR]
Compute a derived measure through the engine and print its cells, optionally
under a named scenario's overrides.
.TP
.B stream \fI<db> <target-measure>\fR...
Read input-cell edits from standard input, one per line, as
.IR "<measure> <value> [Cat=Item,...]"
(the measure by id or name; no coordinate for a scalar measure). Each line is
applied to a live incremental engine, a delta rather than a re-evaluation,
and every cell of a target measure that changed is printed to standard output
as
.IR "<measure> <Cat=Item,...> = <value>" .
A malformed line is reported on standard error and skipped. At end of input
the final state is saved back to
.IR <db> .
Use it to put Improv in a pipeline:
.BR "producer | improv stream m.db Revenue | consumer" .
.TP
.B export \fI<db>\fR
Print the whole model as pretty-printed JSON.
.TP
.B import-sql \fI<db> <source.sqlite> <measure-id> <measure-name> <SELECT> <value-col> <dim-col:cat-id:cat-name>...\fR
Import a SQLite query into a new input measure. Each
.I dim-col:cat-id:cat-name
maps a result column to a category (distinct values become items); the
.I value-col
column supplies the numeric measure value. SQL data enters as ordinary input
cells.
Add
.B \-\-refresh
.RI manual | on-load | interval: SECS
to record when
.B serve-refresh
re-runs the query.
.TP
.B refresh-sql \fI<db> <source.sqlite> <measure-id>\fR
Re-run an SQL-backed measure's stored query and replace its cells with the
fresh result (new dimension values become new items).
.TP
.B refresh-all \fI<db>\fR [\fIsource.sqlite\fR]
Refresh every externally sourced measure at once: all
.B CALL(...)
measures, plus every SQL-backed measure when a source is given.
.TP
.B serve-refresh \fI<db>\fR [\fIsource.sqlite\fR] [\fB\-\-tick\fR \fIsecs\fR]
Run until interrupted, refreshing on each tick (default 5 s) the measures
whose refresh policy is due:
.B on-load
once,
.BI interval: N
every
.I N
seconds. SQL measures refresh only when a source is given.
.TP
.B import-csv \fI<db> <file.csv> <measure-id> <measure-name> <value-col> <dim-col:cat-id:cat-name>...\fR [\fB\-\-tsv\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-header\fR]
Import a CSV or TSV file into a new input measure. Columns are header names
(0-based indices with
.BR \-\-no\-header ).
The delimiter is a comma unless
.B \-\-tsv
is given or the file ends in
.BR .tsv .
.TP
.B export-csv \fI<db> <target.csv> <measure-id>\fR [\fB\-\-tsv\fR]
Write a measure's cells (an input measure's stored cells, or a derived
measure's computed ones) to a CSV or TSV file: one column per dimension
category plus the value column, header row first. Text that a spreadsheet
would run as a formula is prefixed with a quote.
.TP
.B export-sql \fI<db> <target.sqlite> <measure-id> <table> <value-col>\fR
Write a measure's input cells to a SQLite table (one column per dimension
category plus the value column; the table is created if absent). Identifiers
are validated and values bound as parameters.
.TP
.B help
Show usage. Also accepted as
.B \-\-help
and
.BR \-h .
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version. Also accepted as
.BR \-V .
.SH FORMULAS
A derived measure's formula is an expression over measures, written
.BR "Target = expression" :
operators
.BR "+ - * / ^ & %" ,
comparisons
.BR "== <> < <= > >=" ,
.BR "AND OR NOT" ,
and 681 built-in functions with Excel's names
.RB ( ROUND ,
.BR PMT ,
.BR XLOOKUP ,
.BR NORM.DIST ,
\&...).
An aggregation reduces a measure along a category:
.BR "SUM(Sales OVER Month)" ;
.B WHERE
filters it, a window
.B OVER Month[FIRST..THIS]
makes a running total, and
.B INTO
lays a list result along a category.
.BR LET ,
.B LAMBDA
and named LAMBDAs
.RB ( "define db 1 \(dqMargin = LAMBDA(p, c, p - c)\(dq" )
are supported. Every function is listed, with its arguments, in the
Function Reference of the user guide (docs/src/functions.md); the language
is described in docs/src/formulas.md.
.SH EXAMPLES
Build a small Time \(mu Product model:
.PP
.RS
.nf
improv init          model.db
improv add-category  model.db 1 Time
improv add-category  model.db 2 Product
improv add-item      model.db 10 1 2025
improv add-item      model.db 20 2 WidgetA
improv add-measure   model.db 100 Price    number input
improv add-measure   model.db 101 Quantity number input
improv set           model.db 100 10 --at Product=WidgetA
improv set           model.db 101 100 --at Time=2025,Product=WidgetA
improv save-view     model.db 1 Prices 100 --rows Product --cols Time
improv list          model.db
improv show          model.db 100
improv export        model.db
.fi
.RE
Pipe new data through a model and print what it changes:
.PP
.RS
.nf
improv define model.db 102 \(dqRevenue = Price * Quantity\(dq
printf \(aqPrice 15 Product=WidgetA\en\(aq | improv stream model.db Revenue
.fi
.RE
.PP
Work on a model stored in Postgres:
.PP
.RS
.nf
improv list \(aqpostgres://me@127.0.0.1/sales?model=plan\(aq
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <db>
The model store: a single SQLite file created and read by
.BR improv ,
or a
.BI postgres:// ...?model= name
URL. An empty string names an in-memory database that is discarded on exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B IMPROV_LOCALE
The formula locale for
.B define
and
.BR add-derived ,
else the OS locale
.RB ( LC_ALL ,
.BR LC_MESSAGES ,
.BR LANG ).
In German
.RB ( de )
a formula may use the German function names, a decimal comma and
.B ;
between arguments:
.BR "WENN(Price > 1,5; RUNDEN(Price; 2); 0)" .
The English spelling is always accepted; models store it.
.TP
.B IMPROV_EXTFN_TRUSTED=1
Run external functions without the sandbox.
.TP
.B IMPROV_EXTFN_ALLOW_UNSANDBOXED=1
Without bubblewrap, run external functions under resource limits only
(otherwise they are refused).
.TP
.B MENTAT_MMAP_SIZE
Bytes of the store file SQLite may memory-map;
.B 0
turns mapping off.
